whether on chain or otherwise, in other markets?

230
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:27,160
Or do you think there will be institutionalized on ramping off ramping?

231
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:28,760
Which will happen first?

232
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:31,600
That's a really good question. Depends on which markets.

233
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:33,480
You think it'll be different in each one?

234
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:35,520
I think it'll depend on every market.

235
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:38,080
Also, I think we were talking about 2015.

236
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:39,440
Technologies move so much.

237
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:43,840
I want to say 70% of my personal financial transactions happen online now,

238
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:46,400
as opposed to actually physical interactions.

239
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:51,240
So if you don't have off ramping in the physical world, not so critical now.

240
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:53,520
But again, it depends on which country and which region.

241
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:57,480
I'm sure some of the emerging markets having vendors and off-run providers on the ground,

242
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:00,840
little kiosks everywhere, will be critical for mass adoption.

243
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:03,080
But that's just my take. Who knows?

244
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:06,320
Yeah, it's interesting because it's always hard to imagine here in the US.

245
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:10,440
And I guess the reason for that is because each leg of our transaction is handled.

246
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:14,880
We have Venmo for peer-to-peer, and we have payment at a storefront.

247
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:19,320
But getting cash is still arguably a bit more of a pain than it should be in my life, for example.

248
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,280
I don't know if you've the same experience.

249
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:24,280
I don't remember the last time you used cash to me, honestly.

250
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:28,640
But again, I mean, you're removing cash from your financial transactions completely in the US

251
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:30,520
and in some of the more developed world.

252
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:33,040
But cash is king in a lot of countries even now.

253
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:34,840
And so in those countries where cash is king

254
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:37,440
and where you're actually doing a lot of daily transactions in cash,

255
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:40,880
you still need upfront providers that will go from any web-free currency

256
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:46,760
or even stablecoin to cash, so that at least having that as an option is important to on-board users.

257
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:52,240
And then eventually, when all your transactions and all your financial transactions go online or on-chain,

258
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,320
then it won't matter.

259
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:56,520
I've also learned that consumer behavior changes very quickly

260
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:59,240
if it's convenient and inexpensive.

261
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:04,560
If it reduces cost, time, effort, consumer behavior is easier to change than you would think.

262
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:08,080
Definitely, especially if there's not a great alternative.

263
00:13:08,080 --> 00:13:14,560
So given that experience set, did Celo find you or did you find Celo or how did that come about?

264
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:17,280
I tell people this job was made for me.

265
00:13:17,280 --> 00:13:23,240
So I was exploring Web3 and I did this fellowship program that GetCoin used to run called Kernel.

266
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:26,320
And one of the co-founders of Celo came to give an intro to Celo.

267
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:28,640
Just what he thought about Web3, he wasn't even talking about Celo.

268
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:31,280
He was talking about Web3 and the Mycelium network and things like that.

269
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:34,280
If you've ever heard steps, he's one of the co-founders of Celo.

270
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:38,440
If you've ever heard any of his talks, you will learn Mycelium and how everything is interconnected

271
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:42,760
and how financial systems are all interconnected and how we need to generate this whole new world

272
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:45,680
where we think differently from the existing structures.

273
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:49,680
And so he came on and I was like, well, that's a really good way to think about financial systems

274
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:52,240
and how we can reimagine financial systems.

275
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:55,840
And the Celo team was also doing this Kernel cohort.

276
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:58,800
And so I reached out to them once he came on and I was like, oh, I'd love to learn more.

277
00:13:58,800 --> 00:13:59,840
And they were like, oh, cool.

278
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:03,520
That week fatted and they were like, oh, you have a bunch of developing country experience.

279
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:06,520
And we're looking for someone, would you consider interviewing?

280
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:08,560
And the rest, as I say, is history.

281
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:09,560
It's a great fit.

282
00:14:09,560 --> 00:14:13,280
I know the regions that we target really well and work in those regions.

283
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:14,920
So it's like a really great fit.

284
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:21,040
My Mycelium exposure is the extent of a Netflix documentary called Fantastic Fun Guy,

285
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:25,200
but I'll have to get up to speed on the financial network side of it.

286
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:29,240
He describes it similar to how financial networks could be like the fungi network,

287
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:30,840
how it's all interconnected.

288
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:32,120
Yeah, I buy that.

289
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:33,920
It's an interesting conversation.

290
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:39,080
So what was Celo's mission when you joined and how has it evolved or stayed the same?

291
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:43,000
So we're very mission focused chain and our mission is prosperity for all.

292
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,400
And I don't think we've changed the mission.

293
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:48,920
So when you think about prosperity for all, everybody has a different take on this.

294
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:50,400
And you could have prosperity for all.

295
00:14:50,400 --> 00:14:54,760
The way I kind of understand it is prosperity for all translates into financial inclusion,

296
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:56,400
just because I'm a finance background.

297
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:58,560
To me, I take it in from a financial perspective.

298
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:02,080
But prosperity for all from a different perspective is also very refined.

299
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:08,360
And so the Celo mission is essentially build and design for real world adoption of Web 3

300
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:11,040
that unlocks this mission of prosperity for all.

301
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:14,280
And when you think about real world adoption, because the chain,

302
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:17,960
we thought about emerging markets as a really big part of the world.

303
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:21,360
The chain is built in a way that is focused on inclusivity.

304
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:23,160
So we're very mobile first.

305
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:26,440
So all applications of Celo are mobile focused.

306
00:15:26,440 --> 00:15:28,360
We have things such as fee abstraction.

307
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:31,960
So gas in the ecosystem can be paid in stable coins.

308
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:35,760
Because we understand my first crypto transaction didn't go through.

309
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:38,840
And I was sweating because I was like, oh, why is this not going through?

310
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:41,680
And then I realized, oh, I need another token for gas.

311
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,760
But if you are in this ecosystem or when you're onboarding to this ecosystem,

312
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:48,360
let's suppose you have only one wallet and the stable coin on that wallet,

313
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:50,720
you should be able to do the transaction with just the stable coin.

314
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:52,240
And that's the thought behind Celo.

315
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:55,800
OK, you can have one wallet with just a USDT or a USDC.

316
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:58,640
And then the transaction will go through because you can pay for gas.

317
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:00,400
So fee extraction is a really big thing.

318
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:03,360
There's ultra green money, which is inbuilt into the chain,

319
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:08,120
which is essentially like portion allocation of the gas fee goes towards carbon offsetting.

320
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:10,080
Like I mentioned, phone number mapping is really important

321
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:12,480
because we think most of the world interacts with technology

322
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:15,840
through their mobile phone as opposed to like a browser or a laptop.

323
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:19,840
The chain is built for, like I said, everyday use, everyday user.

324
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:22,040
But also the technology is really frontier.

325
00:16:22,040 --> 00:16:24,160
We were the first POS chain.

326
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:27,560
And where are this really cool intersection of frontier tech and real world use case?

327
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:30,440
And I think that's where Celo will always remain.

328
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:32,960
I'm curious how you guys look at the balance,

329
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:36,280
given you're in the stable coin space at a global level,

330
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:40,800
between trying to provide better native systems for people globally

331
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:45,040
versus someone say there's value to offering dollars to people globally

332
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:48,520
as an alternative to their local financial systems.

333
00:16:48,520 --> 00:16:50,400
So how do you strike the balance

334
00:16:50,400 --> 00:16:54,000
and which if one of the two is more mission aligned with what you guys are doing?

335
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:55,520
That's a really good question.

336
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:59,680
I don't think we are picking a side because I don't think there's a side to be picked.

337
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:03,760
You need dollars in the world and access to dollars is really important

338
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:05,880
for users in specific countries.

339
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:08,280
Nigeria, for instance, is a really good example for that

340
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:11,160
where your currency kind of fluctuates on a weekly basis.

341
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:14,000
It's a 20-30% fluctuation on a weekly basis.

342
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,760
All of us talk about Bitcoin to be the store of value,

343
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:20,240
but actually for some people in the world, dollars are the store of value.

344
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:23,520
So having the nominated stable coins is really important in the ecosystem.

345
00:17:23,560 --> 00:17:26,600
And so like I mentioned, we have native USDC and USDT

346
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:29,480
and CUSD as dollar-denominate stable coins

347
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:31,840
that are all gas currencies in the ecosystem.

348
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:34,240
But it's interesting when you provide access to dollars,

349
00:17:34,240 --> 00:17:37,840
then a lot of really interesting cool applications get built on top of it.

350
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:39,560
And then users then want to go,

351
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:41,800
okay, so if dollars are store of value,

352
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:43,840
well, we actually need to do everyday transactions.

353
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:47,000
And so you need their local currencies for everyday transactions.

354
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,640
So currently in the ecosystem, we have, I think, 22 plus native stable coins.

355
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:55,360
And then I think that's between 12 to 14 different currencies.

356
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:57,280
And so a user can store value in dollar,

357
00:17:57,280 --> 00:18:00,280
but then can move that to their local currency

358
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:02,720
and then use it for everyday transactions.

359
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:05,720
And then the other thing that I think most of us don't think about is,

360
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:10,040
if you just have dollars in any ecosystem, then how does lending happen?

361
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:14,560
Because if you're in markets where your local currency devaluates on a regular basis,

362
00:18:14,560 --> 00:18:16,960
then not only are you paying interest on that loan,

363
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:18,680
you're also paying currency fluctuation,

364
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:20,520
which becomes really expensive sometimes.

365
00:18:20,520 --> 00:18:25,720
So definitely no local currencies for some of these interesting use cases like lending.

366
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:30,200
Have you historically faced any pushback from nations or local regulators

367
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:33,760
when you're potentially offering dollars to people globally?

368
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:34,400
Not yet.

369
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:38,120
We're also committed to working with jurisdictions in every region that we operate

370
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:40,600
and we prioritize for compliance and benefits of users.

371
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:43,960
But to be honest, we are not a stable coin issuer per se.

372
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:47,400
We're just an ecosystem where stable coins that are issued by other entities

373
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:49,320
live within our ecosystem.

374
00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:50,520
Yeah, it makes sense.

375
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:53,080
We're also really committed to making sure that we do our due diligence

376
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:55,400
when anything launches in our ecosystem

377
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:59,480
to make sure that they are talking to regulators or in specific conversations with them.

378
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:04,360
Why do you think non-USD stablecoins haven't emerged at broader scale yet?

379
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:06,800
Depends on what you think is broader scale.

380
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:10,920
So firstly, there were not so many non-USD stablecoins a year ago.

381
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:14,040
I think a lot more issuers are issuing non-USD stablecoins.

382
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:18,040
Within the last six to 12 months, I've seen a lot more non-USD stablecoins.

383
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:21,400
I also think that what is the product market fit for non-USD stablecoins

384
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:23,720
is lending and the FX market.

385
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:27,880
And for some of this to be true, it becomes a world in which you have to make sure

386
00:19:27,880 --> 00:19:31,760
